  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "L1 gold-context records for the transferable training contexts report absolute or systemic oral bioavailability estimates at or above 20% (cilomilast 96%, moxifloxacin 91%, ketanserin 49–60%, norfloxacin mean 40%, 5-HTP 49%, bevantolol 57%, rufinamide 50–85%, sulpiride ~35%, doxifluridine ~50–80%/high systemic availability), providing direct endpoint support for F≥20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C03","C04","C05","C06","C07","C08","C09","C10","C11","C12","C13","C14","C15","C16","C17"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L2 same-context records corroborate these estimates (cilomilast tablet 103%, moxifloxacin F=0.971 and 86%, bevantolol ~60%, doxifluridine 34–47% and 50–80%, 5-HTP 40%, rufinamide food-effect increase).",
      "card_ids": ["C18","C19","C20","C21","C22","C23","C24","C27","C28"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records show measurable human Cmax/AUC after oral dosing for carboxylic-acid-containing analogs, consistent with oral absorption; none affirmatively indicates F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C29","C30","C31","C32","C33","C34","C35","C36","C37","C38","C39","C40"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall data are mixed: C59 shows very low Papp and high efflux for a zwitterionic uracil-dione acid analog and C58 shows saturable efflux in a close analog, but other uracil-dione/carboxylic-acid analogs show moderate A-B permeability (C55/C60, C63/C64, C62/C54). These analog-level data do not affirmatively establish F<20% for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C59","C58","C55","C60","C63","C64","C62","C54"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic clearance/metabolic stability records show low-to-moderate intrinsic clearance in human liver microsomes/hepatocytes for retrieved analogs (C65 <10, C66 1.7, C67 <14, C68 22, C71 <10, C73 12, C75/C76 18), providing no affirmative evidence of high first-pass loss; low-to-moderate clearance is consistent with limited presystemic metabolism.",
      "card_ids": ["C65","C66","C67","C68","C71","C73","C75","C76"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C65","C66","C67","C68","C69","C70","C71","C72","C73","C74","C75","C76"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No query-specific absolute oral bioavailability, matched oral-versus-IV, or human PK data exist.",
    "No query-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic-stability measurement exists; L6 records are for structurally distinct analogs and mostly HLM/CYP assays that may not capture UGT-mediated clearance of the carboxylic acid.",
    "No query-specific Caco-2 permeability, efflux-ratio, or intestinal-metabolism measurement exists; L5 negative records are analog-level.",
    "Query dose, formulation, and prandial state are unspecified; several training records show dose/food dependence.",
    "Physicochemical liabilities (full ionization at pH 7.4, logD -1.11, TPSA 119.85, MW 508) create uncertainty but are not affirmative evidence for F<20%."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Direct L1/L2 oral-bioavailability records for the transferable training contexts uniformly support F≥20%, L3 oral exposure is consistent, and L6 adds low-to-moderate hepatic intrinsic clearance for analogs, which does not support high first-pass loss. L5 efflux/permeability concerns for a zwitterionic uracil-dione acid analog and the query's unfavorable ionized-acid physicochemical profile keep confidence low, but they do not amount to affirmative evidence of F<20%. Prediction remains high."
}

